SUBSECTIONS: list[dict[str, Any]] = [
    {
        "subsection_key": "s4_recognize",
        "section_key": "s4_three_forms",
        "title": "Recognizing the three forms",
        "sort_order": 1,
        "intro_html": (
            "<p>Before you dig into features, practice a quieter skill: "
            "<strong>name the form from the equation’s shape</strong>.</p>\n"
            "<p>Look for the pattern:</p>\n"
            "<ul>\n"
            "<li>Powers written out as \\(ax^2+bx+c\\) → <strong>standard form</strong></li>\n"
            "<li>A product of linear factors \\(a(x-r)(x-s)\\) → <strong>factored form</strong></li>\n"
            "<li>A squared binomial plus a constant \\(a(x-h)^2+k\\) → <strong>vertex form</strong></li>\n"
            "</ul>\n"
            "<p>Same parabola underneath — different algebraic packaging. Naming the form "
            "tells you which features you can read immediately.</p>"
        ),
    },
    {
        "subsection_key": "s4_vertex",
        "section_key": "s4_three_forms",
        "title": "Vertex form",
        "sort_order": 2,
        "intro_html": (
            "<p>In vertex form \\(y=a(x-h)^2+k\\), the turning point of the parabola — its "
            "peak or low point — is written into the equation as the <strong>vertex</strong> "
            "\\((h,k)\\).</p>\n"
            "<p>The sign of \\(a\\) tells you whether that vertex is a maximum (opens down) or "
            "a minimum (opens up). Watch the Explore resources, then read vertices, ranges, "
            "and max/min values from equations written this way.</p>"
        ),
    },
    {
        "subsection_key": "s4_factored",
        "section_key": "s4_three_forms",
        "title": "Factored form & zeros",
        "sort_order": 3,
        "intro_html": (
            "<p>In factored form \\(y=a(x-r)(x-s)\\), the numbers \\(r\\) and \\(s\\) are the "
            "<strong>zeros</strong> (the \\(x\\)-intercepts): inputs where the output is zero.</p>\n"
            "<p>The axis of symmetry sits halfway between those zeros. Use the Explore below "
            "to connect roots and sketching, then practise reading zeros and the axis from "
            "factored equations.</p>"
        ),
    },
    {
        "subsection_key": "s4_standard",
        "section_key": "s4_three_forms",
        "title": "Standard form",
        "sort_order": 4,
        "intro_html": (
            "<p>Standard form \\(y=ax^2+bx+c\\) is the expanded polynomial look. The constant "
            "\\(c\\) is immediate: it is the <strong>\\(y\\)-intercept</strong> — the output when "
            "\\(x=0\\).</p>\n"
            "<p>The leading coefficient \\(a\\) still tells opening direction and steepness, but "
            "the vertex and zeros usually take more work from this writing alone. That is why "
            "you keep the other forms nearby.</p>"
        ),
    },
    {
        "subsection_key": "s5_expanding",
        "section_key": "s5_convert_forms",
        "title": "Expanding",
        "sort_order": 1,
        "intro_html": (
            "<p>You’ve been expanding for a while now — multiplying binomials, clearing "
            "brackets, writing products as ordinary polynomials. We’re staying with it "
            "here on purpose: expanding is a key useful skill, and it’s the natural "
            "starting point for everything that follows. Factoring is expanding in reverse, "
            "and later rewrites (toward vertex form) lean on being comfortable moving "
            "between a product and \\(ax^2+bx+c\\).</p>\n"
            "<p>Expanding is the “multiply it out” move. When a curved relationship is "
            "written as a product — factored form, or a squared piece in vertex form — "
            "expanding unpacks it into the familiar \\(y=ax^2+bx+c\\) look.</p>\n"
            "<p>That’s useful when you want the starting height (the \\(y\\)-intercept) "
            "or when a tool expects the ordinary polynomial shape. Expanding doesn’t "
            "change the story; it just shows a different page of the same book.</p>"
        ),
    },
    {
        "subsection_key": "s5_factoring",
        "section_key": "s5_convert_forms",
        "title": "Factoring",
        "sort_order": 2,
        "intro_html": (
            "<p>Factoring is expanding’s reverse: you start from a polynomial and rebuild "
            "it as a product. Why care? Factored form makes the zeros obvious — the inputs "
            "where the output hits zero. In life stories, those zeros are often the moments "
            "something lands, empties, or breaks even.</p>\n"
            "<p>Most factoring mistakes aren’t “bad algebra” so much as skipping a decision. "
            "Use the flowchart below as a deliberate checklist, not a magic spell:</p>\n"
            '<figure class="learning-figure">'
            '<img src="assets/factoring-flowchart.png" '
            'alt="Factoring: Putting it all Together — decision flowchart for GCF, '
            'difference of squares, trinomials, and grouping" '
            'width="960" height="786" loading="lazy" />'
            "<figcaption>Factoring flowchart — start at the top and follow the yes/no "
            "questions until you land on a method (or STOP).</figcaption>"
            "</figure>\n"
            "<p>Always ask first: is there a common factor? Pulling a GCF out front makes "
            "everything else smaller and cleaner — like simplifying a recipe before you "
            "cook.</p>\n"
            "<p>Then count the terms. Two terms often means difference of squares: "
            "\\(a^2-b^2=(a-b)(a+b)\\). Three terms? Check for a perfect-square trinomial, "
            "then a simple \\(x^2+bx+c\\) pair search (numbers that multiply to \\(c\\) and "
            "add to \\(b\\)), then harder cases with decomposition/grouping when the leading "
            "coefficient isn’t 1. Four terms often point to grouping.</p>\n"
            "<p>Not every expression factors nicely over the integers you know — and that’s "
            "okay. The flowchart’s job is to help you try the right tool before you assume "
            "you’re stuck. Watch the Explore resources, then work the examples with the "
            "chart open beside you.</p>"
        ),
    },
    {
        "subsection_key": "s5_convert_cts",
        "section_key": "s5_convert_forms",
        "title": "Converting between forms · Completing the square",
        "sort_order": 3,
        "intro_html": (
            "<p>Sometimes the question you care about isn’t “when does this hit zero?” — "
            "it’s “where’s the peak or low point?” Vertex form "
            "\\(y=a(x-h)^2+k\\) answers that immediately: the vertex is \\((h,k)\\). "
            "Completing the square is the purposeful rewrite that gets you there from "
            "standard form.</p>\n"
            "<p>Here’s the core idea, patiently: you rearrange \\(x^2+bx\\) by adding and "
            "subtracting \\((b/2)^2\\) so a perfect square appears. That isn’t a trick for "
            "its own sake — you’re building the squared chunk that sits at the heart of "
            "vertex form. When \\(a\\neq 1\\), factor \\(a\\) out of the \\(x\\) terms first, "
            "complete the square inside, then distribute carefully.</p>\n"
            "<p>There’s a second conversion path that lives next to completing the square: "
            "symmetry. If you already know two zeros, the axis of symmetry is halfway "
            "between them, and that axis is the vertex’s \\(x\\)-coordinate. Factor → "
            "midpoint of zeros → evaluate for the \\(y\\)-coordinate. Same destination as "
            "completing the square, different road.</p>\n"
            "<p>Choose on purpose. Need zeros? Factor. Need the turning point and you don’t "
            "have clean zeros? Complete the square. Have zeros already? Symmetry may be "
            "faster. Either way, you’re not collecting techniques for a test checklist — "
            "you’re choosing the writing that makes the life question (peak, landing, start) "
            "easier to see.</p>\n"
            "<p>The Explore below walks completing the square; the examples practice both "
            "routes so you can match the method to the question you’re actually asking.</p>"
        ),
    },
]

